-------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe range of repetition in reduced decompositions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/345126130_The_range_of_repetition_in_reduced_decompositions)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; We resolve a conjecture of Hodges–Yong by showing that their spherical permutations are characterized by permutation pattern avoidance. ... We develop the technique of reduced word manipulation to give a range of results concerning reduced words and permutations more generally. ... Given a permutation w, we show that the number of repeated letters in a reduced decomposition of w is always less than or equal to the number of 321- and 3412-patterns appearing in w.\nArticle\n\n# The range of repetition in reduced decompositions\n\n  * January 2021\n  * Advances in Applied Mathematics 122:102107\n\nDOI:10.1016/j.aam.2020.102107\n\nAuthors:\n\nBridget Eileen Tenner\n\nBridget Eileen Tenner\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the author.\n\n## Abstract\n\nGiven a permutation w, we look at the range of how often a simple reflection σk appears in reduced decompositions of w. We compute the minimum and give a sharp upper bound on the maximum. That bound is in terms of 321- and 3412-patterns in w, specifically as they relate in value and position to k. We also characterize when that minimum and maximum are equal, refining a previous result that braid moves are equivalent to 321-patterns.\n\n\n## Citations (9)\n\n## References (20)\n\n... In this work, we characterize boolean prisms in two ways: first using the language of reduced words in Section 2, and then using the language of permutation patterns in Section 3. View\n\nShow abstract\n\nRepetition in reduced decompositions\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jun 2011\n\n  * Bridget Eileen Tenner\n\nGiven a permutation w, we show that the number of repeated letters in a reduced decomposition of w is always less than or equal to the number of 321- and 3412-patterns appearing in w. Moreover, we prove bijectively that the two quantities are equal if and only if w avoids the ten patterns 4321, 34512, 45123, 35412, 43512, 45132, 45213, 53412, 45312, and 45231.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nDerangement Frequency in the Boolean Complex\n\nArticle\n\n  * Apr 2011\n\n  * Kari Ragnarsson\n  * Bridget Eileen Tenner\n\nIn previous work, we associated to any finite simple graph a particular set of derangements of its vertices. These derangements are in bijection with the spheres in the wedge sum describing the homotopy type of the boolean complex for this graph. Here we study the frequency with which a given derangement appears in this set.\n\nView\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) 6$-regular partitions: new combinatorial properties, congruences, and linear inequalities (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/368180429_6-regular_partitions_new_combinatorial_properties_congruences_and_linear_inequalities)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; 6(n) be the set of 6-regular partitions λ of n such that λ has at least ... above reverses the parity of the number of even parts of a partition. ... It is easy to see that Conjecture 3 is a weaker version of Conjecture 2.\n>0,\n\nwith strict inequality if n>k(3 k+ 1)/2.\n\nIt is easy to see that Conjecture 3 is a weaker version of Conjecture 2. The\n\ninequality given by Conjecture 3 can be rewritten in terms of e\n\nP k(n) as follows:\n\nfor n>0, k > 0,\n\n∞\n\nX\n\nj=−∞\n\n(−1)j e\n\nP kn−3 j(3 j−1)>0,(19)\n\nwith strict inequality if n>k(3 k+ 1)/2. Clearly the inequality (18) implies the\n\ninequality (19).\n\nInspired by Theorem 1.10, for k>0 we investigated the following series:\n\n(q, q 5, q 6;q 6)